          Programs that fault, and the trap of testing them bare          ======================================================Three programs on this disk stop with    Exit code: E_PRCABT(228) Process Aborted    Exception: pid=2 vector=$02 err=#000:102Vector $02 on a 68000 is a bus error: the program read an address the machinewould not give it.  os9exec kills the process, so nothing is printed and theprogram looks simply dead.    firq        FIRQ utility    souper      text utility    sysmem      show system memory useThat list was five until it was checked properly.  `aterm` and `snake` were onit, and both are fine.READ THIS BEFORE BELIEVING ANY OF IT------------------------------------A program run as os9exec's FIRST process has no environment at all -- no TERM,no TERMCAP, no PATH.  Nothing on this disk is used that way.  `SYS/login` iswhat sets TERM and TERMCAP, and 69 programs here read TERMCAP.Run bare, `aterm` and `snake` took bus errors and `cribbage` and `crib`printed "Environment variable TERM not defined" and aborted.  Run inside alogin session -- `bash /dd/SYS/login`, then the program -- all four work:`aterm` prints its banner, `snake` draws its board, and both cribbages askwhether you would like the instructions.So the same missing TERMCAP surfaces as a clean error message in one programand as a bus error in another, depending on whether its terminal librarychecks the pointer before following it.  A bus error is not evidence that aprogram is broken.  It may only be evidence that you ran it wrong.Host environment variables do not help: setting TERM in the shell that startsos9exec changes nothing, because os9exec builds the OS-9 environment itself.Going through `SYS/login` is the only way.The three that fault before their first system call---------------------------------------------------`firq`, `souper` and `sysmem` fail in a login session exactly as they do bare,and they fault at the same place in the same shared startup code, on the sameinstruction:    MOVEA.L (A0,$004c),A4      A0 = $AAAAAAA4They have made no system call at all when it happens -- os9exec reports`Last syscall: START`.  $AAAAAAA4 is not a value any program computed; it isos9exec's own fill pattern for memory and registers it has not set.  So thesethree read a pointer out of A0 at entry, and under os9exec A0 holds fill.Whether OS-9/68000 is specified to hand a program anything in A0 at entry is aquestion this collection could not settle from the manuals it has, so thefinding is left as measured rather than assigned.  If A0 is documented ascarrying a pointer, this is an os9exec gap and the three programs are sound;if it is not, they are reading a register they had no right to and were alwaysbroken.  Either way, do not treat "runs under os9exec" as the test thatdecides it.`sysid`, `sysmax` and `sysmin` are from the same suite and do run, so thesuite as a whole is not the problem -- it is this one code path.Two that want floating-point hardware-------------------------------------Separate failure, separate cause.  These two execute floating-pointinstructions with no coprocessor underneath:  os9lib    prints `NON-IMPLEMENTED FLOATING POINT INSTRUCTION` and stops  config    prints char, short, int, long, pointer and FLOAT properties            correctly, then aborts on vector $0B -- the 68000's line-F            emulator vector, which is how a coprocessor instruction announces            that there is no coprocessor.  It dies where `double` begins.Microware's `fpu` module is the floating-point emulator for a machine with no68881, and `p2init` is what installs it as a system extension.  Neither is onthis disk; the permission this collection asked for and received covers cio,math, math881, csl and csl020.  These two programs are the concrete case forasking about `fpu` as well.Five SNOBOL4 games that fault the same way as each other--------------------------------------------------------  poker  blackjak  rpoem  rstory  stoneAll five come from one archive -- `effo_snobol`, Robert Heller's SNOBOL4-in-Clibrary, EFFO forum disk 9 -- and all five fail identically, bare and in asession:    Error #255:255 (E_???) <<unknown error code>>    Illegal instruction: 4afc at 000516c8`$4AFC` is the 68000 ILLEGAL opcode and also the OS-9 module magic number, sothe program has jumped into a module header.  `rstory2` and `tformat` are fromthe same archive and both run, which makes this a bug in the shared libraryrather than in the disk.  Complete K&R source for all seven is in`SRC/effo_snobol`, and `DOC/snobol` holds the author's readme and the patternfiles.  Somebody with the source could find this.About aterm-----------`aterm` works.  It is 100% 68000 assembly, and the collection now ships itsconfiguration:    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl     control file, 20 sequences, ANSI/VT-100 codes    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Phone   empty dialler list, ready to fill in    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Print   printer scriptThe control file was written from the example in the program's own manual, withthe VT-52 escape sequences of the original replaced by ANSI ones and the devicedriver name set to `term`.  `DOC/aterm/ATerm.Doc` is the full manual and`SRC/aterm/ATerm.a` the complete source.  To do anything useful it still wantsa real serial port: `aterm /t1`.